{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":638792,"num_turns":35,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"97ed7069-822d-4d80-aacf-b67e8ab667f4","total_cost_usd":3.159265,"usage":{"input_tokens":400575,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":46142,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":401018,"outputTokens":46167,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":3.159265,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"Done. `kernel.py` and `solution.json` are in place, with all 16 workloads passing.\n\n## What the problem actually is\n\nBehind the MoE-backward framing, the reference reduces to one elementwise expression:\n\n```\nout[i,j] = grad_expert_mask[i,j] + (grad_tokens_per_expert[j] + c)\n```\n\nwhere `c` is a scalar derived from `grad_load_balance_loss` and `training`. Everything else the reference is handed — `topk_idx` (except its row count), `expert_mask`, `tokens_per_expert` — is dead. It's a pure streaming bias-add: read N×256 fp32, write N×256 fp32.\n\n## Kernel\n\nA single Triton pass, replacing the reference's clone → add → broadcast-add chain (3 passes over the data plus 2 host syncs) with one fused pass. Three things mattered:\n\n- **No host sync.** The reference calls `.item()` twice, costing ~27 µs of device sync — more than the entire kernel for most workloads. I read both scalars on-device instead.\n- **Index by masking, not division.** `n_routed_experts` is 256, a power of two, so the broadcast index is `offs & 255`. The 1 KiB vector stays cached across the whole grid.\n- **Rounding fidelity.** The scalar term is computed in fp64 then rounded to fp32, mirroring how torch handles `float32_tensor + python_float`. Result is **bit-exact** (`max_abs = 0.0`) on all 16 workloads, not merely within tolerance.\n\n## Results\n\nAll 16 pass, 2.6×–6.9× over reference. The workloads split into two regimes:\n\n- **Large (n ≥ 32768):** genuinely bandwidth-bound at **6.6–7.4 TB/s** against MI355X's 8 TB/s peak — 83–92% of the hardware bound. Little headroom remains here.\n- **Small (11 of 16 workloads):** pinned at ~8 µs, which is **Triton's launch overhead, not GPU work**. I confirmed this: an empty kernel launch costs the same ~12.6 µs in my harness. I tried the low-level `CompiledKernel.run` path, constexpr-specialization to cut argument count, and plain torch — none beat it, since torch's cheaper dispatch is offset by needing multiple launches or a sync.\n\n## One thing worth flagging\n\nAttempt 3 tested a finer per-size config ladder that looked better in offline benchmarking. On the harness it was **worse** — workloads 9 and 10 regressed (0.047→0.070 ms, 0.016→0.021 ms). The offline differences I'd tuned against were within run-to-run noise. I reverted to the coarse split and re-verified to confirm the recovery, and left a comment in `_pick_config` recording it so the finer ladder doesn't get re-attempted. The submitted version is the measured best, not the best-looking.\n\nNote that scoring is against a Speed-of-Light bound, and for the small workloads that bound assumes negligible launch overhead — so those cases will likely score well below the large ones regardless of kernel quality, since ~8 µs of the ~8 µs is framework dispatch.","ttft_ms":6813,"ttft_stream_ms":6812,"time_to_request_ms":81,"type":"result","duration_ms":721909,"uuid":"84f7eb27-05bd-4a8b-8eff-cdc99186277b"}
